========================================================================================
 Nightly batch spike visibility, in SPL
========================================================================================
 The question these answer: a request-rate spike is showing up around midnight every
 night. Is this the scheduled batch jobs doing what they always do, or is tonight
 different? On-call cannot tell those apart from a raw rate graph, so the spike gets
 ignored, and an ignored alert is a broken alert.

 The fix is not to suppress the spike. It is to draw "normal for this hour" next to it,
 so an abnormal spike is visually obvious and a normal one is visibly normal.

 STATUS: verified 2026-09-02. All 7 searches executed against Splunk 10.4.3 and
 returned rows. Whether they show a MEANINGFUL spike depends entirely on your data: if
 your estate has no nightly batch window, the baseline is flat and that is the correct
 answer, not a broken search.

 A FLAT LINE IS A VALID RESULT. If your estate has no scheduled batch window, the
 hour-of-day baseline is flat and that is the correct answer. These panels are worth
 nothing until you have enough whole days of history to build a baseline from: run the
 density check first, and do not read a trend off two days of data.

 Assumes the sample loaded as index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit with KV_MODE=json.
 Against your own estate the index and sourcetype will differ. Only the first line of
 each search changes.

 Nested JSON fields are referenced as 'request.path' (single quotes) inside eval/where,
 and as request.path=... in the base search.
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9.0  Sanity check: is there enough density to have an hour-of-day shape at all?
       RUN THIS FIRST. A baseline averaged over N days is meaningless if the window
       holds only a handful of events per day. events_per_day well under a few thousand
       means every panel below is averaging noise.

       Also check hours_covered is 24. If your retention window is shorter than a full
       day the "baseline" is only the hours you happen to have.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit type=response
    earliest=-30d@d latest=@d
| eval day = strftime(_time,"%F"), hour = strftime(_time,"%H")
| stats count AS events, dc(day) AS days, dc(hour) AS hours_covered,
        min(_time) AS earliest, max(_time) AS latest
| eval events_per_day = round(events/days, 0),
       earliest = strftime(earliest,"%F %T"),
       latest   = strftime(latest,"%F %T")


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9.1  THE BASELINE: average request rate by hour-of-day
       This is the reference curve every other panel compares against. Averaged over
       whole days only (earliest=-30d@d latest=@d), because a partial trailing day
       drags the hours it does not cover down toward zero and bends the curve.

       stdev comes along for free here and it is what turns "higher than average" into
       "outside normal variation". A midnight batch window that always runs is a high
       mean with a LOW stdev, which is exactly the signature of boring and expected.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit type=response
    earliest=-30d@d latest=@d
| bin _time span=1h
| stats count AS requests BY _time
| eval hour = strftime(_time,"%H")
| stats avg(requests) AS baseline_rate,
        stdev(requests) AS baseline_stdev,
        max(requests) AS observed_max,
        count AS days_sampled
        BY hour
| eval baseline_rate  = round(baseline_rate, 0),
       baseline_stdev = round(baseline_stdev, 0),
       upper_normal   = round(baseline_rate + 2*baseline_stdev, 0)
| sort hour

/*  Read the batch window straight off this table: the hours where baseline_rate is
    high AND baseline_stdev is low are the scheduled work. Consistency is the tell.
    An hour with a high mean and a high stdev is not a schedule, it is intermittent
    load, and it will never be a clean alert no matter how you threshold it.        */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9.2  THE PANEL: today overlaid on the baseline
       The one panel this requirement exists for. Two series on one hour-of-day axis:
       what normally happens, and what is happening now.

       Structure note: appendcols is deliberately NOT used here. It joins by row
       position, so if today has no events at all in some hour that row silently
       shifts and every subsequent hour is compared against the wrong baseline. The
       append + stats pattern below keys on `hour`, so a missing hour stays missing.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit type=response
    earliest=-30d@d latest=@d
| bin _time span=1h
| stats count AS requests BY _time
| eval hour = strftime(_time,"%H")
| stats avg(requests) AS baseline_rate, stdev(requests) AS baseline_stdev BY hour

| append [
    search index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit type=response
        earliest=@d latest=now
    | bin _time span=1h
    | stats count AS today_rate BY _time
    | eval hour = strftime(_time,"%H")
    | fields hour today_rate
  ]

| stats max(baseline_rate)  AS baseline_rate,
        max(baseline_stdev) AS baseline_stdev,
        max(today_rate)     AS today_rate
        BY hour
| eval baseline_rate  = round(baseline_rate, 0),
       upper_normal   = round(baseline_rate + 2*baseline_stdev, 0),
       lower_normal   = round(max(0, baseline_rate - 2*baseline_stdev), 0)
| sort hour
| fields hour today_rate baseline_rate lower_normal upper_normal

/*  Chart as a line chart. today_rate against baseline_rate, with lower_normal and
    upper_normal as the band. Hours where today_rate sits inside the band are the
    answer to "is this the batch job", and it is an answer on-call can read in one
    second without knowing anything about the schedule.

    Hours later than now() have no today_rate. Leave them null rather than filling
    zero: a zero draws a line down to the axis and reads as an outage.               */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9.3  Same comparison via timewrap: day over day, real clock time
       timewrap is the terser way to get "today vs the last 7 days" and it keeps real
       timestamps rather than folding to an hour-of-day axis. Use this one when the
       question is "was LAST night different from the night before", and use 9.2 when
       the question is "what is normal for this hour".

       timewrap is a Splunk core search command; no app needed. It must follow a
       timechart, and the search time range must cover every period being wrapped,
       hence earliest=-8d@d for 7 series plus today.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit type=response
    earliest=-8d@d latest=now
| timechart span=1h count AS requests
| timewrap 1d series=short

/*  Output is one column per day (s0 = today, s1 = yesterday, ...) on a shared
    hour axis. The nightly spike shows as all series rising together at the same hour.
    A real incident shows as ONE series departing from the bundle, which is the
    visual on-call actually needs.                                                   */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9.4  WHO drives the nightly spike: auth methods and identities, batch window only
       Attribution. Once the spike is confirmed normal, this is the panel that names
       the scheduled jobs causing it, so the next person does not have to rediscover
       them. Adjust the hour window to whatever 9.1 showed as the batch hours.

       The night_share column is the point. An identity at ~100% night_share runs
       ONLY in the batch window, which makes it a scheduled job. An identity near the
       overall traffic share is just normal load that happens to also run at night.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit type=response
    earliest=-30d@d latest=@d
| eval hour = tonumber(strftime(_time,"%H"))
| eval window = if(hour >= 23 OR hour < 2, "night", "day")
| eval who        = coalesce('auth.metadata.service_account_name', 'auth.display_name', "unknown"),
       method     = 'request.mount_type',
       source_ip  = 'request.remote_address'
| stats count AS total,
        count(eval(window=="night")) AS night_requests,
        dc(source_ip) AS source_ips,
        values(method) AS methods,
        max(_time) AS last_seen
        BY who
| eval night_share = round(100 * night_requests / total, 1),
       last_seen   = strftime(last_seen,"%F %T")
| where night_requests > 0
| sort - night_requests
| head 50

/*  Cross-check with the auth-method traffic search in stale-secrets.spl. If the
    night spike is one auth method it is almost always one platform's scheduler.      */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9.5  WHICH paths the batch window touches
       Follows 9.4: having named the identity, name what it reads. Feeds directly into
       the stale-secret searches, because a batch job hammering a path is also the reason that path never looks
       dormant.

       The coalesce on the namespace is mandatory. The root namespace carries
       request.namespace.path as an EMPTY STRING, and a bare concatenation with it
       yields null, dropping every root-namespace event with no error at all.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit type=response
    request.mount_type=kv request.path="*/data/*"
    earliest=-30d@d latest=@d
| eval hour = tonumber(strftime(_time,"%H"))
| where hour >= 23 OR hour < 2
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| eval who = coalesce('auth.metadata.service_account_name', 'auth.display_name', "unknown")
| stats count AS night_requests, dc(who) AS distinct_callers, values(request.operation) AS ops
        BY secret
| sort - night_requests
| head 50


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9.6  DEVIATION FROM BASELINE: the alertable form
       This is what a detection rule should fire on, not a raw rate threshold. A fixed
       "alert above N requests/hour" threshold is precisely what the batch spike breaks:
       set it above the nightly peak and you are blind all day, set it below and it
       pages every midnight.

       z is the deviation in standard deviations for THIS hour of the day, so the same
       single number works at 03:00 and at 14:00.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit type=response
    earliest=-30d@d latest=now
| bin _time span=1h
| stats count AS requests BY _time
| eval hour = strftime(_time,"%H")
| eventstats avg(requests) AS baseline_rate, stdev(requests) AS baseline_stdev BY hour
| eval baseline_stdev = if(baseline_stdev == 0 OR isnull(baseline_stdev), 1, baseline_stdev)
| eval z = round((requests - baseline_rate) / baseline_stdev, 2)
| eval verdict = case(
      z >=  3, "ABNORMAL: far above normal for this hour",
      z >=  2, "elevated for this hour",
      z <= -3, "ABNORMAL: far below normal, check for an outage",
      true(),  "within normal range for this hour")
| eval baseline_rate = round(baseline_rate, 0)
| table _time hour requests baseline_rate z verdict
| sort - _time

/*  Two things to get right before this becomes an alert:

    1. The baseline window includes the hour being scored, so a large sustained spike
       slowly teaches the baseline that it is normal. For a real detection, compute the
       baseline from a summary index built on a LAGGED window (see 1.4 for the
       scheduled-search-plus-collect pattern) and score today against it.

    2. Thirty days is a guess. Pick a window that covers the real schedule: if the heavy
       batch runs monthly, a 30 day baseline sees it roughly once and its stdev is
       garbage. Consider adding day-of-week to the BY clause, since weekend traffic on
       most estates is a different curve entirely.

    Chart z as a bar chart. Zero is normal, and the nightly spike sits flat at zero,
    which is the entire point of the requirement.                                     */
